// What this package is, what it covers, and the decisions that shape
// every rule in it. Rule files follow lib/CodingRule.
export anchor CodingStandards:
    purpose:
        A library of rules taken from well-known coding standards. Each rule
        is one Piton spec. Other projects tether this package and pick the
        rules they want into their own instructions and skills.
    scope:
        - Language style guides, such as PEP 8, the Google style guides, the Airbnb JavaScript guide, Effective Go and the Rust API Guidelines
        - Safety and security standards, such as SEI CERT C, C++ and Java, and the OWASP Secure Coding Practices
        - Language-neutral principles, such as SOLID, the Twelve-Factor App and the Clean Code heuristics, and the C++ Core Guidelines
        - Design and review guidance, such as A Philosophy of Software Design, The Pragmatic Programmer, the code smells of Refactoring.Guru and Martin Fowler, and Google's code review practices
        - The Linux kernel coding style
    nonGoals:
        - Linter rule sets, such as ESLint, Ruff, Pylint or Clippy
        - Paid standards, such as MISRA
        - Sources whose license forbids derivative works or commercial use: BARR-C, The Art of Unix Programming (CC BY-ND) and 97 Things Every Programmer Should Know (CC BY-NC-SA)
        - Code Complete's chapter checklists, which are large, dated and no longer published
        - Merging rules that several standards share into one rule
        - Links between matching rules in different standards
    decisions:
        - Every rule from every included standard is extracted, not a curated subset.
        - One rule is one file, holding one coding-rule anchor.
        - Rules from different standards stay separate, even when they say the same thing.
        - No rule may infringe a source's copyright or license. Rules capture each source's intent in our own words, and code examples are written fresh. Each rule links to its source, so the original wording is one click away.
        - Titles are our own words too, except where the source's license allows reuse with attribution, as CERT's CC BY 4.0 does. CERT titles are kept because they double as the rules' names.
        - Every standard records its license, and an overlap check compares rule text against the fetched sources before a standard is accepted.
        - Severity is filled only when the source gives one. The CERT standards give one; most style guides do not.
        - For the CERT standards, both the rules and the recommendations are extracted. A recommendation is a coding-rule whose first tag is recommendation. They come from the SEI site at cmu-sei.github.io, which replaced the old wiki. Deprecated rules are left out.
        - Where a source has no rule ids, the id is the section number, the section's HTML id or a slug of its heading. OWASP items are numbered from 1 within each section.
        - Sections that only introduce, describe or link elsewhere are not rules. Placeholder rules whose body is only "???" are left out.
        - Each standard lists its rules in source order in its own rules property.
        - Rules from books, such as Clean Code, A Philosophy of Software Design, The Pragmatic Programmer and Fowler's Refactoring, come from each book's own numbered list. Their item lists were checked against public summaries, not the books. Each rule links to the book's page.
        - Advice in a book that is about career, teams or process rather than code has practice as its first tag.
        - Clean Code is taken from the numbered heuristics in chapter 17 of the 1st edition, the book's own rule list. The book is not public, so every rule links to that chapter on O'Reilly.
        - The package ships the coding-standards-audit skill, which checks code against chosen standards and reports each violation with its rule.
        - Anchor names start with the standard's prefix, such as CertC or Pep8, so names never collide across standards.
    openQuestions:
        - Whether the share-alike and copyleft sources should stay: the OWASP standards (CC BY-SA), the GNU Coding Standards (GFDL) and the Linux kernel style (GPL-2.0). Their rules are paraphrased, but share-alike may reach the package.
        - Whether consumers want a shared tag vocabulary across standards. Tags are free-form, lowercase and kebab-case today.
        - Whether the OWASP rules should move to the OWASP Developer Guide, which replaced the archived Secure Coding Practices project.
        - How code examples should show a literal tab. piton format turned leading tabs in escape blocks into spaces, so tab-indentation examples use spaces or are null.
